NMAP NETWORK / WEB SERVICE SCAN REPORT

Assessment Type : Localhost Web Application Enumeration Target :
127.0.0.1 (localhost) Target Port : TCP/3000 Service : ppp? Application
: OWASP Juice Shop Scan Command : nmap -Pn -sC -sV -p 3000 127.0.0.1
Nmap Version : 7.98 Scan Date/Time : 14 September 2026, approximately
06:07 (-04:00 shown by Nmap) Assessment Scope : Local test environment

1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY

An Nmap service/version detection scan was performed against TCP port
3000 on localhost (127.0.0.1). The host was reachable and port 3000 was
found open. The service returned content identifying the application as
OWASP Juice Shop, an intentionally insecure web application used for
security training.

The scan also exposed several HTTP response headers and application
metadata. These observations are useful for further web application
security testing, but the Nmap output alone does not prove an
exploitable vulnerability.

2. SCAN OBJECTIVE

The objective of this scan was to:

-   Identify whether the target host is reachable.
-   Identify open TCP ports on the specified port.
-   Detect the service running on port 3000.
-   Obtain basic service/version information.
-   Run Nmap default scripts (-sC) for additional HTTP enumeration.
-   Collect HTTP response headers and basic application fingerprinting
    data.

3. TARGET INFORMATION

Target IP : 127.0.0.1 Target Hostname : localhost Port Scanned :
3000/tcp Port State : OPEN Detected Service: ppp? (service fingerprint
shown by Nmap) Application : OWASP Juice Shop Host Status : UP Reported
Latency: 0.00010s

4. COMMAND USED

nmap -Pn -sC -sV -p 3000 127.0.0.1

Option Explanation: -Pn : Treat the host as online and skip host
discovery. -sC : Run Nmap’s default NSE scripts. -sV : Detect
service/version information. -p 3000 : Scan only TCP port 3000.

5. KEY SCAN RESULTS

PORT STATE SERVICE 3000/tcp open ppp?

The service on port 3000 responded successfully to HTTP requests.

HTTP GET Request: HTTP/1.1 200 OK

The response body and metadata identified: Application Name : OWASP
Juice Shop Description : “Probably the most modern and sophisticated
insecure web application”

This is consistent with a local OWASP Juice Shop training environment.

6. HTTP SECURITY OBSERVATIONS

6.1 Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * Severity: Informational / Requires
Validation

Observed: Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

This indicates that the application permits cross-origin requests from
any origin for the relevant response. This is not automatically a
vulnerability. Its security impact depends on the endpoints,
authentication mechanism, credentials configuration, and whether
sensitive data can be accessed cross-origin.

Recommended validation: - Test authenticated and unauthenticated
endpoints separately. - Check whether Access-Control-Allow-Credentials
is enabled. - Determine whether sensitive information is returned to
arbitrary origins. - Review CORS configuration against the application’s
actual trust model.

6.2 X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff Status: Positive Security Control

Observed: X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

This header instructs browsers not to MIME-sniff responses and is
generally considered a good security configuration.

6.3 X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN Status: Positive Security Control

Observed: X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

This restricts framing of the application to the same origin and
provides protection against many clickjacking scenarios.

6.4 Referrer-Policy: no-referrer Status: Positive Security Control

Observed: Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

This reduces leakage of the originating URL through the HTTP Referer
header.

6.5 Feature-Policy: payment ‘self’ Status: Informational / Legacy Header

Observed: Feature-Policy: payment ‘self’

The response includes a Feature-Policy header controlling the payment
feature. Modern applications commonly use Permissions-Policy for this
type of control. The actual browser support and security value should be
verified.

6.6 Cache-Control Observed: Cache-Control: public, max-age=0

This indicates public caching is permitted while specifying a
zero-second freshness lifetime. Whether this is appropriate depends on
the sensitivity of the response.

7. HTTP METHODS OBSERVED

An HTTP OPTIONS request returned:

HTTP/1.1 204 No Content

Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, HEAD, PUT, PATCH, POST, DELETE

Security Note: The presence of methods such as PUT, PATCH and DELETE
does not by itself mean that these operations are exploitable.
Authorization and endpoint-specific controls must be tested to determine
whether unauthorized state-changing operations are possible.

Recommended validation: - Enumerate application endpoints. - Test each
method with an authorized account and an unauthenticated request. -
Check authorization enforcement on PUT, PATCH and DELETE. - Confirm
whether CSRF protections are present where applicable.

8. INFORMATION DISCLOSURE

The response exposes application-level information including:

-   OWASP Juice Shop application name.
-   Application description.
-   Framework/application response behavior.
-   HTTP response headers.
-   Request/response capabilities.
-   A feature-policy configuration.
-   An application resource path visible in the fingerprint output.

Severity: Informational

Impact: Exposed technology and application metadata can assist
reconnaissance. However, the information alone is not sufficient to
establish a security vulnerability.

